{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FFJa4Qgetu3ambJYa5Nza6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: Batch fold_ple_from_evals Descriptor Array\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tThe `fold_ple_evals` function in `LogupZerocheckGpu` launches one `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` per matrix per trace in a sequential loop. At APC 300, nsight showed ~797 instances of `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<false>` totaling 9.5ms GPU time (avg 12µs each). The optimization replaces the per-trace loop with a batched descriptor-array kernel: a single launch for all rotate=false operations and a single launch for all rotate=true operations. This was expected to save ~2-3ms in kernel launch overhead and improve SM utilization for small traces.\n6\t\n7\t## Implementation\n8\t\n9\t### CUDA side (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`)\n10\t- Added `FoldPleDesc` struct with src/dst pointers, dimensions, and `block_start` field\n11\t- Added `batched_fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<ROTATE>` using binary search on `block_start` to map 1D blocks to descriptors, then decomposing local block index into (row_block, col_idx) per descriptor\n12\t- Added `_batched_fold_ple_from_evals` C launcher with same block_size/smem logic as existing kernel\n13\t\n14\t### Rust FFI (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`)\n15\t- Added `#[repr(C)] FoldPleDesc` struct with Send+Sync\n16\t- Added extern \"C\" binding and safe wrapper `batched_fold_ple_from_evals`\n17\t\n18\t### Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fold_ple.rs`)\n19\t- Added `FoldPleItem`, `FoldPleResult`, and `batched_fold_ple_evals_rotate` function\n20\t- Collects all items, pre-allocates per-item output DeviceBuffers, builds two descriptor arrays (rotate=false and rotate=true), uploads via `to_device()`, and launches 1-2 batched kernels\n21\t\n22\t### Integration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`)\n23\t- Replaced per-trace `fold_ple_evals_rotate` loop with 3-phase approach: collect all matrices → batch-launch → distribute results\n24\t- mem_limit accounting preserved (common_main is always last per trace)\n25\t\n26\t### Deviations from plan\n27\t- None significant. Plan was followed as written.\n28\t\n29\t## Results\n30\t\n31\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n32\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n33\t| **STARK excl trace APC 300** | 2455ms | 1115ms | 1127ms (1112ms run2) | 2.18x lower | +12ms noise (run2: -3ms noise) |\n34\t| **Round 0 APC 300** | 662ms | 181ms | 181ms (179ms run2) | 3.66x lower | 0ms (run2: -2ms noise) |\n35\t| **LogUp GKR APC 300** | 790ms | 370ms | 370ms (363ms) | 2.14x lower | 0ms |\n36\t| **MLE Rounds APC 300** | 180ms | 162ms | 170ms (166ms) | 1.06x lower | +8ms noise |\n37\t| **STARK excl trace APC 100** | — | 1316ms | 1310ms | — | -6ms noise |\n38\t| **Round 0 APC 100** | — | 168ms | 162ms | — | -6ms noise |\n39\t| **STARK excl trace APC 0** | — | 1891ms | 1810ms | — | -81ms (noise in GKR) |\n40\t| **Round 0 APC 0** | — | 177ms | 177ms | — | 0ms |\n41\t\n42\tAll 94 tests pass. No regression at any APC configuration.\n43\t\n44\t## Assessment\n45\t\n46\tThe optimization did **not** achieve its goal. Round 0 at APC 300 showed 0ms improvement across 3 runs.\n47\t\n48\tThe plan estimated 3-5ms from eliminating ~795 kernel launch overhead cycles. However:\n49\t1. **Per-launch CUDA overhead is ~2-3µs**, confirmed by previous tasks (batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload measured ~1µs per CUDA API call). At 797 launches, total overhead is ~1.6-2.4ms — barely above the measurement noise floor.\n50\t2. **SM utilization gain is negligible**: the fold_ple kernel operates on the default stream (single-threaded section, not multi-streamed), so there's no inter-stream concurrency to improve. The kernel simply runs sequentially either way — batching just removes the CPU→GPU launch gap between kernels, which the CUDA driver pipeline already hides.\n51\t3. **9.5ms total GPU time for 797 kernels** means the average kernel takes 12µs. The launch overhead (~2µs per kernel) is already 83% hidden by kernel execution time via the CUDA driver's kernel pipeline.\n52\t\n53\tThe fundamental issue: `fold_ple_evals` runs on the default stream where kernels execute sequentially. Batching multiple sequential kernels into one larger kernel only saves the launch gap between them, which the CUDA driver already pipelines.\n54\t\n55\t## Future Work\n56\t\n57\t- **fold_ple is not a bottleneck**: At 9.5ms total GPU time (~0.8% of STARK excl trace), fold_ple is too small to yield meaningful gains even with perfect optimization.\n58\t- **Multi-stream fold_ple**: Running fold_ple across multiple streams (like Round 0 and GKR) could provide ~4-5x speedup on the 9.5ms, but the 2ms net gain doesn't justify the complexity.\n59\t- **Focus elsewhere**: The remaining optimization opportunities in STARK excl trace are in kernel execution time (LogUp GKR 370ms, Round 0 181ms, MLE Rounds 162ms), not in CUDA API overhead, which has been thoroughly addressed by previous tasks.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"4689c5f9-3482-4fb0-b4ce-0e99a50f82b4","timestamp":"2026-04-16T03:15:21.606Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0200-batch-fold-ple-descriptor-array/report.md","content":"# Report: Batch fold_ple_from_evals Descriptor Array\n\n## Description\n\nThe `fold_ple_evals` function in `LogupZerocheckGpu` launches one `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` per matrix per trace in a sequential loop. At APC 300, nsight showed ~797 instances of `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<false>` totaling 9.5ms GPU time (avg 12µs each). The optimization replaces the per-trace loop with a batched descriptor-array kernel: a single launch for all rotate=false operations and a single launch for all rotate=true operations. This was expected to save ~2-3ms in kernel launch overhead and improve SM utilization for small traces.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### CUDA side (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`)\n- Added `FoldPleDesc` struct with src/dst pointers, dimensions, and `block_start` field\n- Added `batched_fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<ROTATE>` using binary search on `block_start` to map 1D blocks to descriptors, then decomposing local block index into (row_block, col_idx) per descriptor\n- Added `_batched_fold_ple_from_evals` C launcher with same block_size/smem logic as existing kernel\n\n### Rust FFI (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`)\n- Added `#[repr(C)] FoldPleDesc` struct with Send+Sync\n- Added extern \"C\" binding and safe wrapper `batched_fold_ple_from_evals`\n\n### Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fold_ple.rs`)\n- Added `FoldPleItem`, `FoldPleResult`, and `batched_fold_ple_evals_rotate` function\n- Collects all items, pre-allocates per-item output DeviceBuffers, builds two descriptor arrays (rotate=false and rotate=true), uploads via `to_device()`, and launches 1-2 batched kernels\n\n### Integration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`)\n- Replaced per-trace `fold_ple_evals_rotate` loop with 3-phase approach: collect all matrices → batch-launch → distribute results\n- mem_limit accounting preserved (common_main is always last per trace)\n\n### Deviations from plan\n- None significant. Plan was followed as written.\n\n## Results\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| **STARK excl trace APC 300** | 2455ms | 1115ms | 1127ms (1112ms run2) | 2.18x lower | +12ms noise (run2: -3ms noise) |\n| **Round 0 APC 300** | 662ms | 181ms | 181ms (179ms run2) | 3.66x lower | 0ms (run2: -2ms noise) |\n| **LogUp GKR APC 300** | 790ms | 370ms | 370ms (363ms) | 2.14x lower | 0ms |\n| **MLE Rounds APC 300** | 180ms | 162ms | 170ms (166ms) | 1.06x lower | +8ms noise |\n| **STARK excl trace APC 100** | — | 1316ms | 1310ms | — | -6ms noise |\n| **Round 0 APC 100** | — | 168ms | 162ms | — | -6ms noise |\n| **STARK excl trace APC 0** | — | 1891ms | 1810ms | — | -81ms (noise in GKR) |\n| **Round 0 APC 0** | — | 177ms | 177ms | — | 0ms |\n\nAll 94 tests pass. No regression at any APC configuration.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThe optimization did **not** achieve its goal. Round 0 at APC 300 showed 0ms improvement across 3 runs.\n\nThe plan estimated 3-5ms from eliminating ~795 kernel launch overhead cycles. However:\n1. **Per-launch CUDA overhead is ~2-3µs**, confirmed by previous tasks (batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload measured ~1µs per CUDA API call). At 797 launches, total overhead is ~1.6-2.4ms — barely above the measurement noise floor.\n2. **SM utilization gain is negligible**: the fold_ple kernel operates on the default stream (single-threaded section, not multi-streamed), so there's no inter-stream concurrency to improve. The kernel simply runs sequentially either way — batching just removes the CPU→GPU launch gap between kernels, which the CUDA driver pipeline already hides.\n3. **9.5ms total GPU time for 797 kernels** means the average kernel takes 12µs. The launch overhead (~2µs per kernel) is already 83% hidden by kernel execution time via the CUDA driver's kernel pipeline.\n\nThe fundamental issue: `fold_ple_evals` runs on the default stream where kernels execute sequentially. Batching multiple sequential kernels into one larger kernel only saves the launch gap between them, which the CUDA driver already pipelines.\n\n## Future Work\n\n- **fold_ple is not a bottleneck**: At 9.5ms total GPU time (~0.8% of STARK excl trace), fold_ple is too small to yield meaningful gains even with perfect optimization.\n- **Multi-stream fold_ple**: Running fold_ple across multiple streams (like Round 0 and GKR) could provide ~4-5x speedup on the 9.5ms, but the 2ms net gain doesn't justify the complexity.\n- **Focus elsewhere**: The remaining optimization opportunities in STARK excl trace are in kernel execution time (LogUp GKR 370ms, Round 0 181ms, MLE Rounds 162ms), not in CUDA API overhead, which has been thoroughly addressed by previous tasks.\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":60}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LhZjKpJuEUiqSzuxmqmCJk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the optimization history. Let me take stock:\n\n**Current State** (from log.md and recent reports):\n- Baseline STARK excl trace at APC 300: 2455ms\n- Current best STARK excl trace at APC 300: ~1110-1115ms (2.21x lower)\n- Goal: >2x improvement (already achieved, but more is better)\n\n**Key remaining components at APC 300** (from latest measurements):\n- LogUp GKR: ~359-370ms (largest component)\n- Trace Commit: ~196ms\n\n\n- Round 0: ~181ms\n- MLE Rounds: ~162-166ms\n- Openings: ~176-204ms (WHIR at ~130ms, Stacked Reduction at ~75ms)\n\nFrom the 43 previous optimization tasks, I've learned that multi-stream parallelism works well for Round 0 and GKR input eval with 8 threads, batch descriptor arrays help with MLE rounds and stacking scatter but cause issues with Round 0 and GKR input eval, and increasing VPMM page size showed promise but was cut short. 16 MiB gave huge gains - a local optimum. Pre-allocation patterns work for small buffers but fail at larger sizes, while pingpong buffer reuse helps with MLE fold and GPU-side polynomial extraction handles Round 0 well. CUDA API overhead is mostly exhausted at ~0.5-1µs per call, and kernel execution time is now the dominant bottleneck - MLE Rounds spend about 113ms of 166ms just executing kernels rather than launching them. Round 0 batching has an unsolved kernel hang bug affecting AIRs.\n\nI've tried many approaches that haven't panned out: batch kernels cause either hang bugs or L2 cache thrashing, multi-stream strategies either hit kernel-bound limits or disrupt pool state, warp-level optimizations run into memory bandwidth walls, and various other tweaks like VPMM threshold changes and pipeline draining haven't yielded improvements. The precompute work is already overlapped, and grid sizing isn't the actual bottleneck here.\n\nNow I need to establish a baseline, measure current performance, profile to identify the real constraints, research what's causing the bottlenecks, brainstorm solutions, pick the most promising one, and work through a structured review loop. Let me check the current state of the repository and see what measurements already exist.\n\nThe git history shows the last batch-fold-ple optimization was reverted, so I'm back to the pre-optimization state. I should verify the current metrics files are up to date and check if the GPU is available before running fresh measurements.\n\nLet me first look at the existing measurement files to see how recent they are, then I'll need to run the pairing benchmarks in the powdr repo for different APC values and analyze them with spec.py.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8775,"cache_read_input_tokens":39667,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8775},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"65fbf867-9855-43d1-9d6d-492cfb4e3239"}
