=======\n4. NTT & MERKLE TREE OPERATIONS WITHIN WHIR\n================================================================================\n\nNTT OPERATIONS:\n\n1. Initial batch_ntt_small (line 145)\n   └─ Chunk-wise iDFT for algebraic batching coefficient extraction\n   └─ Size: height * D_EF elements\n   └─ Kernel: Radix-2 FFT in reverse order (inverse transform)\n\n2. Per-round batch_expand_pad (line 299)\n   └─ Expand polynomial coefficients to RS domain\n   └─ Input: g_coeffs (f_height elements)\n   └─ Output: g_rs (codeword_height elements where codeword_height > f_height)\n   └─ Kernel: Padding + coefficient reordering\n\n3. Per-round batch_ntt (line 308)\n   └─ Forward FFT for RS codeword evaluation\n   └─ Input: g_rs padded coefficients\n   └─ Output: g_rs in evaluation form\n   └─ Kernel: Radix-2 FFT forward\n\nMERKLE TREE OPERATIONS:\n\n1. Tree Construction (line 318)\n   └─ MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash<HS::MerkleHash>()\n   └─ Input: g_rs matrix (codeword_height x D_EF)\n   └─ Layer stride: 1 << k_whir\n   └─ Output: Tree with authenticated root hash\n\n2. Root Commitment (line 324)\n   └─ tree.root() returns HS::Digest\n   └─ Observed in transcript for Fiat-Shamir\n\n3. Polynomial Evaluation at z_0 (line 332)\n   └─ eval_poly_ext_at_point_from_base(g_coeffs, f_height, z_0)\n   └─ Input: g_coeffs in coefficient form\n   └─ Output: Single EF value = g(z_0)\n\n4. Batch Query Merkle Proofs (lines 400, 447)\n   └─ batch_query_merkle_proofs(trees, query_indices)\n   └─ Operation: For each query index, generate authentication path\n   └─ Output: MerkleProof<HS::Digest> per query\n\n5. Batch Open Rows (lines 419, 452)\n   └─ batch_open_rows(backing_matrices, query_indices, query_stride, rows_per_query)\n   └─ Operation: Extract matrix rows at queried positions\n   └─ Output: Vector<Vector<EF or F>> = opened values\n\n================================================================================\n5. MEMORY ALLOCATION PATTERNS & VPMM PAGE SIZE REGRESSION\n================================================================================\n\nKEY ALLOCATION POINTS IN WHIR:\n\nPer-Execution Allocations:\n  f_ple_evals:     height * D_EF * 8 bytes         [height * 4 * 8]\n  w_moments:       (2^m) * 32 bytes                [2^m * 32]\n  d_s_evals:       2 * 32 bytes                    [64 bytes]\n  d_sumcheck_tmp:  tmp_buffer_capacity * 32 bytes  [variable]\n\nPer-Sumcheck-Round Allocations (k_whir rounds per WHIR round):\n  new_f_coeffs:    (f_height / 2) * 32 bytes\n  new_w_moments:   (f_height / 2) * 32 bytes\n  d_sumcheck_tmp:  (resized if needed)\n\nPer-RS-Round Allocations (1 per WHIR round):\n  g_coeffs:        f_height * D_EF * 8 bytes      [f_height * 4 * 8]\n  g_rs:            D_EF * codeword_height * 8 bytes (if not last round)\n\nPer-Query-Round Allocations:\n  d_z0_pows2:      log_height * 32 bytes           [small]\n  d_z_pows2:       num_queries * log_height * 8 bytes [medium]\n\nVPMM PAGE SIZE CHANGE IMPACT:\n\nCommit 4863c316 changed default from 2MB to 16MB (8x granularity)\n\nMemory manager routing (cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:75-92):\n  Before (2MB page):\n    if size < 2MB:         use cudaMallocAsync\n    else (>= 2MB):         use VPMM (cuMemCreate + mapping overhead)\n  \n  After (16MB page):\n    if size < 16MB:        use cudaMallocAsync\n    else (>= 16MB):        use VPMM\n\nAFFECTED ALLOCATIONS (for m=16, height=2^16=65536):\n\n1. f_ple_evals: 65536 * 4 * 8 = 2.1 MB\n   ├─ BEFORE: VPMM path → cuMemCreate overhead (~0.4ms per call)\n   └─ AFTER: cudaMallocAsync path → faster but different pool behavior\n\n2. w_moments: 65536 * 32 = 2.1 MB\n   ├─ BEFORE: VPMM path\n   └─ AFTER: cudaMallocAsync path\n\n3. g_rs (per round): 4 * 65536 * 8 = 2.1 MB\n   ├─ BEFORE: VPMM path\n   └─ AFTER: cudaMallocAsync path\n\n4. d_sumcheck_tmp: Typically 256K-512K elements = 8-16 MB\n   ├─ Borderline allocation\n   └─ May fall into different paths depending on grid size\n\nWHY THIS CAUSES REGRESSION (100ms → 128ms = +28%):\n\n1. cudaMallocAsync Pool Fragmentation\n   ├─ Repeated allocation/deallocation of 2-4MB buffers\n   ├─ Small allocations may fragment the cudaMallocAsync memory pool\n   └─ Pool search/coalescing overhead increases latency\n\n2. Loss of VPMM Defragmentation Benefits\n   ├─ VPMM has active defragmentation between rounds\n   ├─ VPMM can coalesce free regions across allocations\n   ├─ cudaMallocAsync pool is simpler, less intelligent\n   └─ Repeated alloc/free cycles become less efficient\n\n3. Stream Ordering & Synchronization Points\n   ├─ cudaFreeAsync is stream-ordered (not immediately freed)\n   ├─ Repeated cudaMallocAsync calls in tight loops\n   ├─ May encounter pool contention or require implicit syncs\n   └─ Adding up to ~28ms overhead per proof\n\n4. L2 Cache & Memory Bandwidth\n   ├─ Larger allocation patterns shift data in different cache patterns\n   ├─ Previous 2MB pages → VPMM physical paging\n   ├─ New 16MB threshold means more allocations bypass physical paging\n   └─ Different memory access patterns and TLB behavior\n\nSOLUTION INDICATORS:\n  ├─ Pre-allocate sumcheck buffers to avoid repeated cycles\n  ├─ Use VPMM_PAGE_SIZE env var to override (e.g., 4MB sweet spot)\n  ├─ Batch allocations into fewer, larger chunks\n  ├─ Add memory pooling within WHIR logic\n  └─ Consider k_whir=1 to reduce allocation frequency\n\n================================================================================\n6. SUMMARY - KEY FILES & LINE RANGES\n================================================================================\n\nCore WHIR Implementation:\n  File: crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs\n  ├─ Lines 56-531: prove_whir_opening_gpu() - main entry point\n  ├─ Lines 100-189: Initialization phase\n  ├─ Lines 89-137: Algebraic batching phase\n  ├─ Lines 168-515: Main WHIR rounds loop\n  └─ Lines 197-517: Per-round operations\n\nCUDA FFI Wrappers:\n  File: crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/whir.rs\n  ├─ Lines 12-71: whir_algebraic_batch_traces()\n  ├─ Lines 80-105: whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round()\n  ├─ Lines 112-132: whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments()\n  └─ Lines 140-160: w_moments_accumulate()\n\nCUDA Kernels:\n  File: crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu\n  ├─ Lines 24-74: whir_algebraic_batch_traces_kernel\n  ├─ Lines 83-136: whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round_kernel\n  ├─ Lines 141-166: whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments_kernel\n  ├─ Lines 196-221: w_moments_accumulate_kernel\n  ├─ Lines 227-240: _whir_algebraic_batch_traces launcher\n  ├─ Lines 246-278: _whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round launcher (two-phase)\n  ├─ Lines 280-293: _whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments launcher\n  └─ Lines 295-309: _w_moments_accumulate launcher\n\nGPU Backend Integration:\n  File: crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs\n  ├─ Lines 122-189: OpeningProver trait implementation\n  ├─ Lines 133-188: prove_openings() - WHIR entry from backend\n  └─ Line 184: Call to prove_whir_opening_gpu()\n\nMemory Management & VPMM:\n  File: crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs\n  ├─ Lines 71-99: d_malloc() - routing logic based on page_size\n  └─ Lines 104-120: d_free()\n\n  File: crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs\n  ├─ Lines 36-87: VpmmConfig - page size configuration\n  ├─ Lines 150-246: VirtualMemoryPool initialization\n  ├─ Lines 159-178: Page size resolution logic\n  └─ Default: 8 * granularity (8x minimum, typically 16MB)\n\n================================================================================","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VsFWvH1ZUbtcR8rKDCrN4x","session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"bdd6f378-67d8-471d-b09d-55f0e01acdf9","timestamp":"2026-04-16T03:22:25.757Z"}
