n't address unless we change how memory is allocated or managed.\n\nBut when I tried routing more allocations back through VPMM, it regressed across other phases like LogUp GKR and MLE Rounds. The 16MB boundary appears to be a local optimum where moving it in either direction causes problems elsewhere. Instead of adjusting the allocation threshold, what if I pad WHIR's buffer sizes up to 16MB so they naturally route through VPMM without changing the global threshold?\n\nThis would waste about 42MB per WHIR instance, which is negligible given the 24GB GPU and current peak usage around 15GB. The tradeoff is simple to implement but the regression might stem from something other than allocation routing, so there's medium confidence this actually solves it. The VPMM path itself involves additional overhead from cuMemCreate and cuMemMap calls that could factor in.\n\nNow I'm working through the math on whether padding to 16MB would actually trigger VPMM routing based on the pool threshold logic, since that's the crux of whether this optimization would even work.\n\nThe solution is straightforward: round up WHIR buffer allocations to at least 16MB so they route through VPMM like the baseline, which should recover most of that 28ms regression with minimal risk and no side effects on other allocations.\n\nNow I'm verifying this was actually the cause by checking the performance timeline—the Openings phase did increase by 28ms after the VPMM change, and since Stacked Reduction stayed constant at ~75ms, that confirms WHIR is where the slowdown happened. I want the optimization to have real impact—at least 20ms—and I need to be confident it'll work. Let me consider if there's a better approach than padding WHIR buffers.\n\nWhat if I move the CPU NTT operations in Stacked Reduction Round 0 to the GPU using batch_ntt_small? Looking at the reconstruct_s0_from_g function, it's doing a D2H transfer, then several CPU operations on tiny polynomials (size 16-32), which probably takes less than 0.1ms total. The 36ms overhead attributed to this section suggests the bottleneck isn't the compute itself.\n\nActually, the real issue is likely GPU kernel execution in the Round 0 loop, not the CPU reconstruction. So padding WHIR allocations to force VPMM routing still seems like the best bet, though 28ms might not feel like a \"significant\" win.\n\nBut wait—the VPMM change actually improved LogUp GKR by 175ms and Trace Commit by 50ms, so it was net positive overall. WHIR was the only component that regressed. I should check if there are other places where this change caused similar regressions that I could fix the same way.\n\nLooking at the numbers: 28ms out of 1129ms STARK time is about 2.5%, which is moderate compared to past optimizations. The nice thing about the padding approach is that it's low-risk—just allocation size changes, no complex code rewrites. Now I'm thinking through which WHIR allocations would benefit from this: the per-round allocations like g_rs, the sumcheck temporaries, and the Merkle tree digest layers could all be padded to 16 MiB to route through VPMM instead of cudaMallocAsync.\n\nThe catch is that VPMM has its own overhead—cuMemCreate and cuMemMap each take about 0.4ms, so 5-10 allocations would add 2-4ms. That leaves a net gain of around 25ms if the regression is purely about allocation routing. But I'm realizing the regression might not be that simple. It could be about how WHIR's allocations interact with the rest of the proof's allocations in the cudaMallocAsync pool. Padding WHIR to 16 MiB changes the pool state for the entire proof, and the decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold work showed that routing more allocations through VPMM actually hurt other phases. So this change would reduce the cudaMallocAsync pool density, which could have ripple effects I'm not fully accounting for.\n\nThough to be fair, we're only padding a handful of specific allocations, not changing a global threshold. The pool state for everything else stays the same. And the physical page consumption from 3-4 extra 16 MiB allocations is probably minimal. This feels like the same pool sensitivity issue, but at a smaller scale.\n\nI think I should try this approach and see what feedback comes back. But I'm also wondering if there's something with more impact I should be chasing instead—maybe something around the Trace Commit RS that could move the needle more significantly.\n\nLooking at the RS encoding breakdown, the NTT is clearly the bottleneck at around 154ms out of 197ms total. The NTT kernel processes the entire matrix, but what if we could pipeline it by splitting the work into column batches and overlapping the computation with other operations?\n\nIf we process batches sequentially—running NTT on batch 2 while simultaneously doing Merkle hashing on batch 1—we'd overlap the two operations. Since NTT takes ~154ms and Merkle hashing takes ~33ms, the pipeline would theoretically save around 33ms by hiding the hashing time under the NTT computation. But the actual speedup depends on how evenly we can distribute the work across batches and whether the overhead of splitting outweighs the gains.\n\nThe math shows we'd approach 33ms of savings with many batches, but stream switching and memory contention would eat into that in practice, leaving us with maybe 20-30ms of real improvement. Given the complexity of managing the pipeline and the modest payoff, this doesn't seem worth the effort.\n\nI'm going to commit to the WHIR VPMM padding approach instead.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the segment breakdown. The STARK trace exclusion takes 1129ms across 2 segments at APC 300, and if those segments are imbalanced, there might be room to optimize the workload distribution. The spec.py output only gives totals though, so I need to dig into the raw metrics to see if per-segment timing data is available.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_in