{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WLQBcy6YfJSE8XzA1cCK19","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: Pre-allocate WHIR Fold Buffers\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tPre-allocate and reuse WHIR sumcheck fold buffers (`f_coeffs`, `w_moments`) via a pingpong pattern instead of allocating new DeviceBuffers each inner round. Each of the `k_whir × num_whir_rounds` inner sumcheck rounds allocates two fresh DeviceBuffers and frees the previous pair. For the first ~5 rounds at APC 300 (buffer sizes 128–16 MiB), these go through VPMM, creating page churn. The hypothesis was that eliminating per-round VPMM allocations/frees would save 5–10ms on WHIR and stabilize GPU memory pool state for subsequent phases.\n6\t\n7\t## Implementation\n8\t\n9\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`, function `prove_whir_opening_gpu`\n10\t\n11\tTwo changes were made:\n12\t\n13\t1. **Pre-allocate scratch buffers** (after line 195, before the outer loop): Allocated one pair of scratch DeviceBuffers at initial maximum size `1 << m` (256 MiB each at APC 300). These persist across all WHIR outer rounds.\n14\t\n15\t2. **Pingpong for non-final inner rounds**: For inner rounds `0..k_whir-2`, fold output goes to the pre-allocated scratch pair instead of newly allocated buffers, then `std::mem::swap` makes the scratch the \"current\" buffer. The final inner round (`k_whir-1`) still allocates correctly-sized buffers to preserve `.len() == output_height`, which `w_moments_accumulate` (cuda/whir.rs:148) reads to determine processing height.\n16\t\n17\tNo deviations from the plan. All 14 WHIR unit tests pass.\n18\t\n19\t## Results\n20\t\n21\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n22\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n23\t| **STARK excl trace APC 300** | 2455ms | 1117ms | 1115ms | -1340ms, 2.20x lower | -2ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n24\t| **WHIR APC 300** | 100ms | 130ms | 126ms | +26ms, 1.26x higher | -4ms, 1.03x lower |\n25\t| **STARK excl trace APC 100** | 2155ms | 1389ms | 1312ms | -843ms, 1.64x lower | -77ms, 1.06x lower |\n26\t| **WHIR APC 100** | 138ms | 154ms | 156ms | +18ms, 1.13x higher | +2ms (noise) |\n27\t| **STARK excl trace APC 0** | 2153ms | 1784ms | 1810ms | -343ms, 1.19x lower | +26ms (noise) |\n28\t| **WHIR APC 0** | 220ms | 217ms | 221ms | +1ms (noise) | +4ms (noise) |\n29\t\n30\tSecond run at APC 300 confirmed: WHIR 125ms, STARK excl trace 1127ms — consistent with first run.\n31\t\n32\t## Assessment\n33\t\n34\tThe optimization **failed** to meet the rollback criteria:\n35\t\n36\t- WHIR at APC 300 improved by only ~4ms (threshold: 5ms)\n37\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 showed no improvement (-2ms, within noise)\n38\t- No regression at APC 0 (within 26ms noise band, below 20ms systematic threshold)\n39\t\n40\tThe per-round VPMM allocation overhead at APC 300 is lower than estimated. At most 6 VPMM allocations are eliminated (inner rounds 0–2 of WHIR round 0, × 2 buffers), with subsequent rounds using cudaMallocAsync's pool cache (buffer sizes ≤ 8 MiB). The actual per-VPMM-operation overhead is ~0.5–1ms (not ~2ms as estimated), so maximum possible savings were ~3–6ms — and we observed ~4ms. The pool state hypothesis (that eliminating VPMM churn would benefit subsequent bandwidth-bound phases) did not materialize.\n41\t\n42\t## Future Work\n43\t\n44\t- WHIR at APC 300 is 126ms, which is already well-optimized. The dominant cost is GPU kernel execution in the 16 inner sumcheck rounds, not allocation overhead.\n45\t- The WHIR phase has regressed 26ms from baseline (100ms → 126ms) across previous optimizations. This regression is likely due to accumulated VPMM pool state changes from other optimizations (similar to patterns seen in `cache-codeword-buffer-across-segments` and `multistream-stacked-reduction-round0`), not allocation overhead within WHIR itself.\n46\t- Further WHIR improvements would require algorithmic changes (e.g., fusing sumcheck+fold into a single kernel, or reducing the number of inner rounds).\n47\t- The WHIR phase is now a small fraction (2.3%) of total proof time and (11.3%) of STARK excl trace — diminishing returns make further WHIR-specific optimization low priority.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"31a84a8d-017c-42a5-8621-06b340371659","timestamp":"2026-04-16T03:39:05.855Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-2230-prealloc-whir-fold-buffers/report.md","content":"# Report: Pre-allocate WHIR Fold Buffers\n\n## Description\n\nPre-allocate and reuse WHIR sumcheck fold buffers (`f_coeffs`, `w_moments`) via a pingpong pattern instead of allocating new DeviceBuffers each inner round. Each of the `k_whir × num_whir_rounds` inner sumcheck rounds allocates two fresh DeviceBuffers and frees the previous pair. For the first ~5 rounds at APC 300 (buffer sizes 128–16 MiB), these go through VPMM, creating page churn. The hypothesis was that eliminating per-round VPMM allocations/frees would save 5–10ms on WHIR and stabilize GPU memory pool state for subsequent phases.\n\n## Implementation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`, function `prove_whir_opening_gpu`\n\nTwo changes were made:\n\n1. **Pre-allocate scratch buffers** (after line 195, before the outer loop): Allocated one pair of scratch DeviceBuffers at initial maximum size `1 << m` (256 MiB each at APC 300). These persist across all WHIR outer rounds.\n\n2. **Pingpong for non-final inner rounds**: For inner rounds `0..k_whir-2`, fold output goes to the pre-allocated scratch pair instead of newly allocated buffers, then `std::mem::swap` makes the scratch the \"current\" buffer. The final inner round (`k_whir-1`) still allocates correctly-sized buffers to preserve `.len() == output_height`, which `w_moments_accumulate` (cuda/whir.rs:148) reads to determine processing height.\n\nNo deviations from the plan. All 14 WHIR unit tests pass.\n\n## Results\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| **STARK excl trace APC 300** | 2455ms | 1117ms | 1115ms | -1340ms, 2.20x lower | -2ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n| **WHIR APC 300** | 100ms | 130ms | 126ms | +26ms, 1.26x higher | -4ms, 1.03x lower |\n| **STARK excl trace APC 100** | 2155ms | 1389ms | 1312ms | -843ms, 1.64x lower | -77ms, 1.06x lower |\n| **WHIR APC 100** | 138ms | 154ms | 156ms | +18ms, 1.13x higher | +2ms (noise) |\n| **STARK excl trace APC 0** | 2153ms | 1784ms | 1810ms | -343ms, 1.19x lower | +26ms (noise) |\n| **WHIR APC 0** | 220ms | 217ms | 221ms | +1ms (noise) | +4ms (noise) |\n\nSecond run at APC 300 confirmed: WHIR 125ms, STARK excl trace 1127ms — consistent with first run.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThe optimization **failed** to meet the rollback criteria:\n\n- WHIR at APC 300 improved by only ~4ms (threshold: 5ms)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 showed no improvement (-2ms, within noise)\n- No regression at APC 0 (within 26ms noise band, below 20ms systematic threshold)\n\nThe per-round VPMM allocation overhead at APC 300 is lower than estimated. At most 6 VPMM allocations are eliminated (inner rounds 0–2 of WHIR round 0, × 2 buffers), with subsequent rounds using cudaMallocAsync's pool cache (buffer sizes ≤ 8 MiB). The actual per-VPMM-operation overhead is ~0.5–1ms (not ~2ms as estimated), so maximum possible savings were ~3–6ms — and we observed ~4ms. The pool state hypothesis (that eliminating VPMM churn would benefit subsequent bandwidth-bound phases) did not materialize.\n\n## Future Work\n\n- WHIR at APC 300 is 126ms, which is already well-optimized. The dominant cost is GPU kernel execution in the 16 inner sumcheck rounds, not allocation overhead.\n- The WHIR phase has regressed 26ms from baseline (100ms → 126ms) across previous optimizations. This regression is likely due to accumulated VPMM pool state changes from other optimizations (similar to patterns seen in `cache-codeword-buffer-across-segments` and `multistream-stacked-reduction-round0`), not allocation overhead within WHIR itself.\n- Further WHIR improvements would require algorithmic changes (e.g., fusing sumcheck+fold into a single kernel, or reducing the number of inner rounds).\n- The WHIR phase is now a small fraction (2.3%) of total proof time and (11.3%) of STARK excl trace — diminishing returns make further WHIR-specific optimization low priority.\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SfdvUTMJRNLGiXJWiKuSZr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, very important findings from this report:\n\n1. The prealloc-whir-fold-buffers task DID pre-allocate scratch buffers at 256 MiB each (which would go through VPMM). But it only saved 4ms on WHIR.\n\n2. The report states: \"The WHIR phase has regressed 26ms from baseline (100ms → 126ms) across previous optimizations. This regression is likely due to accumulated VPMM pool state changes from other optimizations, not allocation overhead within WHIR itself.\"\n\n3. \"The dominant cost is GPU kernel execution in the 16 inner sumcheck rounds, not allocation overhead.\"\n   \nSo pre-allocating WHIR buffers through VPMM was already attempted and only yielded 4ms of improvement, meaning the remaining 22ms regression stems from pool state degradation rather than WHIR's own allocation patterns. This means I need to abandon the idea of fixing this through WHIR-specific buffer management, since the real issue is systemic pool state sensitivity that pre-allocation alone can't address.\n\nThe core problem is that optimizing allocations in one phase cascades into performance degradation elsewhere, so I can't surgically fix WHIR without risking regressions in other components. I need to explore something fundamentally different.\n\nReducing MLE rounds would help, but that's tied to AIR heights which the compiler controls, not something I can change. What I'm really noticing is that at APC 300, most traces are quite small in the MLE domain and become degenerate after a few rounds, so there might be an opportunity to optimize how we handle these small-height evaluations rather than running the same expensive computation for traces that are effectively already collapsed. degenerate traces is trivial — just multiply by a scalar.\n\nLooking at the MLE eval for the constraint MLE rounds: the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` is called for ALL traces each round (791 instances total). Even degenerate traces get their own kernel launch. If we could skip degenerate traces after they've been folded...\n\nActually, let me check: does the current batched MLE kernel already skip degenerate traces?\n\nLooking at the batch MLE code: the `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel` uses descriptors that include `num_y` (the hypercube dimension). For degenerate traces (num_y = 0 or 1), the kernel does minimal work. But the descriptor is still included, and the kernel launch still happens.\n\nThe real bottleneck is that `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` launches once per trace across all 14 rounds — that's 791 kernel launches total. For degenerate traces where this computation is just a scalar multiplication, we're paying the kernel launch overhead for trivial work. If we could batch all degenerate traces into a single operation instead of launching a kernel per trace, we'd save significant overhead. rounds, that would be 623 × 14 = 8722 instances. But we see only 791 instances. So precompute_logup_combinations is NOT called per-round — it's called once per trace total.\n\nLet me re-check. The nsight shows:\n- precompute_logup_combinations_kernel<true>: 791 instances\n- precompute_logup_combinations_kernel<false>: 791 instances\n\nIf there are 623 AIRs per segment × 2 segments = 1246 traces total, and 791 is less than that. So this might be called for a subset of traces (those that are non-degenerate in the current round).\n\nActually, looking at the logup_zerocheck code, precompute_logup_combinations is called per-trace during the MLE round setup, not per-round. The 791 instances might be the total across all rounds for the non-degenerate traces.\n\nI'm getting confused without seeing the exact MLE round code. Let me look at it. Actually, I've spent a lot of time on this analysis. Let me step back and consider what I haven't tried yet.\n\nI've already ruled out several approaches: WHIR VPMM padding failed to help, batch stacked reduction targets are too small, trace commit is dominated by RS encoding, multi-stream overlap has too many dependencies, and MLE anti-scaling is kernel-bound with batching already applied.\n\nWhat about coalescing per-segment WHIR and stacked reduction processing instead of proving each segment sequentially?\n\nThe stacked reduction and WHIR together take roughly 100ms per segment. If segments 0 and 1 could share preprocessing, that might help — but they have different trace data and challenge vectors, so there's no real sharing opportunity.\n\nHere's another angle: the fold_ple_evals loop in stacked reduction launches one kernel per trace, which at 623 traces means 623+ kernel launches. Nsight shows 797 instances at 11µs average, totaling about 9ms. If I could batch these into a single descriptor-array kernel, I'd eliminate most of those launches and save roughly 1.2ms from launch overhead alone, though the total GPU time is only...\n\nActually, that's not enough savings. Let me think about fusing the WHIR sumcheck and fold instead. Right now each inner round computes s_evals, copies them to the host, updates the transcript, samples a challenge, then folds. But the D2H of those 2 elements is a pipeline drain, and the fold depends on the challenge from the transcript, creating a strict sequential dependency that can't be broken without changing the protocol itself.\n\nI could try batching multiple WHIR rounds on the GPU with transcript processing there too, like the failed gpu-gkr-transcript-processing attempt. But WHIR only has ~4 rounds per outer round versus GKR's ~40, so the per-round overhead might matter more. Still, that earlier analysis showed the CPU work per round was already fast at ~20µs—with 16 inner rounds that's only 320µs total, which is negligible.\n\nI need to think about this differently.\n\nLooking back at all 43 iterations, I'm noticing that almost everything targeted CUDA overhead, kernel launches, and memory allocation. But the actual kernel compute itself has barely been touched. The bottleneck is now clearly in kernel execution time—GKR input eval, constraint kernels, MLE eval, WHIR kernels, and trace commit are all eating up hundreds of milliseconds. These are bandwidth-bound or compute-bound operations that haven't really been optimized at the kernel level. What if I focused on the actual computation instead?\n\nThe Poseidon2 hashing in Trace Commit is one piece—it's running 25 instances with about 9-10ms per instance, using shared memory for Merkle tree reduction. But Poseidon2 is already well-studied and hard to push further. The real opportunity might be in the batch NTT, which dominates both Trace Commit and WHIR costs. Could switching to a faster NTT algorithm make a meaningful difference?\n\nThe current NTT implementation from Plonky3 or ICICLE is already GPU-optimized, but there's room to explore better memory access patterns, more efficient twiddle factor handling, and improved occupancy. For the APC 300 case with 106,842 columns, the batch NTT processes each column independently, which should be highly parallel. The question is whether larger batch sizes or different kernel configurations could squeeze out more performance here.\n\nAfter extensive exploration across 43 iterations, I'm realizing the remaining bottlenecks are fundamentally hard to address. The dominant cost is kernel execution time itself, which resists optimization without changing the algorithm. Phase overlap is blocked by Fiat-Shamir's sequential dependencies, and memory layout is sensitive to allocation patterns I can't easily control. We've already achieved a 2.17x improvement from 2455ms to 1129ms, which is solid progress toward the >2x scaling goal between APC 0 and APC 300.\n\nI keep circling back to multi-streaming the Stacked Reduction Round 0 kernel launches as a potential win, but that approach failed before—the per-thread accumulation buffers created pool state disruption that cost us 176ms in STARK execution time. The core issue is that concurrent kernels would need atomic operations on shared buffers like d_g_pos and d_g_neg, and the previous attempt's workaround of using per-thread accumulation buffers required extra device allocations that ended up being counterproductive.\n\nWhat if I used atomic additions directly instead? The output buffers are tiny—just 48 EF elements total—so atomic operations should be fast enough. The stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0 kernel already does block reduction internally before writing to the output buffer, so making that final write atomic is straightforward. The real question is whether the overlap would actually pay off: Round 0 takes 26ms total across both segments, and even if I could perfectly parallelize it, I'd only save about 11ms at best. That's not compelling enough given the risk of repeating the pool disruption problem.\n\nLet me step back and look at something completely different: the handoff between trace commitment and constraint proving. Right now stacked_commit() builds the RS codeword matrix and Merkle tree, optionally caching the RS matrix, then prove_rap_constraints() reads directly from the original traces instead of the RS matrix. The RS matrix only gets used later during WHIR openings—either it's cached and reused, or it gets recomputed on demand. If I don't cache it, the matrix gets freed after the Merkle tree is built, saving GPU memory but forcing recomputation later.\n\nThe real question is whether the caching strategy is tuned for APC 300. If we're caching the RS matrix when we shouldn't be, we're wasting memory that could help GKR and MLE phases. If we're not caching when we should be, we're paying for recomputation during WHIR. Let me check what the current configuration actually does—the cache_rs_code_matrix flag in the prover config controls this, and I need to see whether it's being set optimally.\n\nLooking at how the Merkle tree handles this: the backing_matrix is either stored in GPU memory or dropped based on the cache flag. For APC 300 with over 100k columns, keeping that matrix resident throughout Constraints and Openings could create memory pressure. But here's an interesting angle—what if we could avoid caching entirely and instead recompute the RS matrix on a separate GPU stream during the Openings phase, overlapping it with the Stacked Reduction work? That way we'd free up memory earlier without paying the full recomputation cost.\n\nThe traces stay in GPU memory throughout the proof, so there's no dependency blocking us from starting RS recomputation concurrently on a different stream. The Stacked Reduction doesn't need the RS matrix, so stream A could handle that while stream B handles the RS encoding in parallel. The question is whether the WHIR code actually triggers this recomputation or if it's pulling from cache...\n\nChecking the WHIR logic...\n\nIf the RS matrix is cached, we skip recomputation entirely. If not cached, we recompute it during round 0. For APC 300, I'd need to verify the prover config, but if we disable caching, we could overlap the recomputation with Stacked Reduction and save roughly 38ms—the duration of the shorter operation. The tradeoff is that the traces need to stay resident in GPU memory and we're adding stream management complexity. So the real question is whether RS matrix caching is currently enabled—if it is, overlapping doesn't help since there's nothing to recompute. If it's disabled, the recomputation is already happening within the 65ms per segment, and overlapping with Stacked Reduction could save around 38ms, though I need to verify how much of that 65ms is actually spent on RS matrix work.\n\nLooking at the baseline numbers, WHIR at APC 300 is 50ms per segment versus the current 64ms per segment. If RS recomputation were happening, WHIR would be significantly slower—closer to 164ms per segment—so the matrix is almost certainly cached already, which means this overlap approach won't help.\n\nLet me shift focus to reducing the Merkle tree queries in WHIR instead, particularly in round 0 where we query the initial Merkle trees for each commit.\n\nFrom the profiling data, I'm seeing `query_digest_layers` and `matrix_get_rows_fp_kernel` as the main query operations, with `grind_kernel` being the proof-of-work grinding rather than the queries themselves. The actual number of queries is locked by security parameters, so I can't reduce that without compromising security.\n\nAfter working through all these angles, I think the most promising approach is batching the degenerate traces in the MLE rounds to reduce the per-trace precompute_logup_combinations overhead through a single scalar multiplication operation.\n\nActually, let me step back and look at the bigger picture here. We've already hit 2.17x speedup on STARK excluding trace at APC 300 after 43 iterations—the system is heavily optimized at this point, and any single optimization will likely yield diminishing returns. Looking at the per-segment breakdown, the constraints phase in segment 0 is consuming 392ms, which is the largest single component, so that's where I should focus.\n\nWithin constraints, LogUp GKR dominates at 216ms. The GKR inner rounds are sequential, with each round processing the entire tree before it shrinks for the next iteration. The first few rounds handle the largest trees and contribute most of the time. Looking at the kernel-level breakdown from the profiling data, the compute_round_and_revert_kernel is taking 70ms across 141 instances, while the precompute_m_build_partial_kernel and multifold_kernel are each around 45-47ms. These kernels in the GKR inner rounds seem like the most promising target for optimization.\n\nThe bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel is taking 50ms total across just 6 instances, which is the initial tree building step that converts GKR input evaluation results into a fractional tree. I'm wondering if we could overlap this tree building with the input