pectively, which includes the stack, RS encoding, and Merkle tree work. The RS encoding itself breaks down into batch expansion and padding at around 5ms, followed by MLE interpolation. I've already explored pre-allocating Merkle tree buffers across rounds, which only yielded 4ms. Instead, I should focus on fusing bit-reversal with the NTT computation in the RS code matrix phase—that could save 4-8ms per segment by eliminating a separate kernel call and reducing memory bandwidth overhead. But I need to verify if `batch_ntt` already supports built-in bit reversal, and honestly I'm overthinking this. Let me just commit to the most realistic win: overlapping the stacked_commit Merkle tree root D2H transfer with the per-AI computation to hide that latency.\n\nActually, that only saves 0.1ms—not worth it. Looking at the phase timing more carefully, there's essentially no dead time between Trace Commit (107ms), Constraints (392ms), and Openings (103ms), so I can't reclaim anything there. The best remaining option is pre-allocating the `d_block_sums` buffer in stacked_reduction at its maximum required size to avoid repeated allocations in the Round 0 inner loop.\n\nChecking the code, the buffer currently grows as needed during the per-trace kernel loop, which means 10-20 allocations per segment at APC 300. Pre-computing the max size upfront and allocating once would save 2-5ms with very low complexity. But honestly, that's still below any meaningful threshold. I need to make a final call here.\n\nGoing with batching the D2H synchronization in MLE rounds instead. Right now each round does separate to_host calls for zerocheck and logup evaluation results, creating two sync points. If I combine both into a single device buffer and copy them together, I eliminate one sync point per round—that's roughly 28 fewer sync points across all segments. Each to_host has fixed overhead from the cudaMemcpyAsync setup and pipeline drain, so consolidating these should give better gains than