{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NTq8Y5AwCtqQJCu6N3P7BG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FQJ8j3tdyUsJhDWUdFWcnp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\n# With grid=262144, block=256 for the revert kernel:\n# elements = num_x >> 1 = total_leaves / 4\n# blocks_needed = ceil(elements / 256)\n# 262144 = ceil(total_leaves / 4 / 256) = ceil(total_leaves / 1024)\n# total_leaves = 262144 * 1024 = 268,435,456 = 2^28 ≈ 268M\n# But that's 268M * 64 bytes = 17.2 GB -- too big for backup on a 24GB GPU\n\n# Wait, maybe the grid isn't fully utilized. The kernel does 'for (idx = idx_base; idx < num_x / 2; idx += stride)'\n# So num_x / 2 = elements could be LESS than grid * block.\n# But frac_compute_round_launch_params computes blocks_needed = ceil(elements / block_size)\n# If blocks_needed = 262144, then elements >= (262144 - 1) * 256 + 1 = 67,108,609\n# So elements is at least ~67M\n# total_leaves = 4 * elements >= 268M\n\n# Actually wait - let me re-read the launch params\n# elements = num_x >> 1\n# num_x is passed as pq_size / 2 from Rust\n# At round = total_rounds - 1, pq_size = 2 << (total_rounds - 1) = total_leaves\n# So num_x = total_leaves / 2\n# elements = total_leaves / 4\n# blocks = ceil(total_leaves / 4 / 256) = ceil(total_leaves / 1024)\n# For blocks = 262144: total_leaves = 262144 * 1024 = 268,435,456\n\n# Actually, let me check: what if total_leaves is smaller but the grid calc differs?\n# blocks_needed = (elements + block_size - 1) / block_size\n# 262144 = (elements + 255) / 256\n# elements = 262144 * 256 - 255 = 67,108,609 (minimum)\n# to elements = 262144 * 256 = 67,108,864 (maximum)\n\n# So total_leaves is in [268,434,436, 268,435,456]\n# Buffer size: 268M * 64 bytes = 17.2 GB\n\n# This makes the backup approach IMPOSSIBLE -- you can't allocate another 17 GB on a 24GB GPU\n\nprint('total_leaves ≈', 262144 * 1024)\nprint('Backup buffer size:', 262144 * 1024 * 64 / (1024**3), 'GB')\n\n# Hmm, but maybe the plan's N ≈ 2M estimate is just for the 'last outer round's tree level'\n# not total_leaves? Let me re-check what the revert undoes.\n\n# Actually, I think I made an error. Let me re-trace:\n# pq_size = 2 << round where round ranges from 1 to total_rounds-1\n# At round = total_rounds - 1: pq_size = 2 << (total_rounds - 1) = 2^total_rounds = total_leaves\n# The revert kernel gets num_x = pq_size / 2 = total_leaves / 2\n# elements = num_x >> 1 = total_leaves / 4\n# blocks = ceil(total_leaves / 4 / 256)\n# For blocks = 262144: total_leaves = 268M\n# Backup: 268M elements * 64 bytes = 17.2 GB\n\n# This is obviously too much. Either:\n# 1. The plan's N estimate is wrong\n# 2. My understanding of the mapping is wrong\n# 3. total_leaves is actually much smaller and the grid isn't from the last round\n\n# Let me look at this from the other direction:\n# The 141 instances across all rounds. For 2 segments with ~20-28 rounds each,\n# outer rounds 1..total_rounds-1 each have 1 revert, plus there might be 3 segments (leaf + internal recursion?).\n# 141 / 3 ≈ 47 rounds per GKR call. total_rounds ≈ 48.\n# total_leaves = 2^48? No, that's absurd.\n\n# OK wait, there could be multiple GKR calls per segment. Let me check.\n# From the metrics: there are GkrLayerSumcheckAir instances across multiple groups\n# (leaf, internal_for_leaf, internal_recursive.0, internal_recursive.1)\n# Each proving phase might have its own GKR fractional sumcheck.\n\n# With 4 GKR calls and about 141/4 ≈ 35 rounds each:\n# total_rounds ≈ 36, total_leaves = 2^36? Still absurd.\n\n# Actually, I bet the grid=262144 instance is NOT from the last outer round.\n# Maybe at APC 300 the tree is much deeper but each outer round uses many elements.\n# Let me think again...\n\n# Actually, I realize: 141 instances with exponentially growing sizes:\n# grid sizes: 1(42), 2(6), 4(6), ..., 262144(2)\n# Grid=1: 42 instances. Each grid=1 corresponds to tiny rounds.\n# If there are 3 GKR calls (leaf recursion might have its own), each with ~20 rounds,\n# that's 60 rounds total. But 141 > 60. So probably more calls.\n\n# The pattern: for each GKR call with total_rounds=T, there are T-1 revert instances\n# with sizes: grid=1 (rounds 1..small), ..., up to the max for that call.\n\n# 141 instances could be: several GKR calls with various total_rounds values.\n# The 2 grid=262144 instances are the largest ones from the 2 largest GKR calls.\n\n# For grid=262144: total_leaves/4 ≈ 67M → total_leaves ≈ 268M\n# This is 17GB, which can't be a single contiguous buffer.\n\n# WAIT: maybe the GKR operates on a folded buffer, not all leaves at once?\n# Let me re-read fractional_sumcheck_gpu's signature.\n\nprint()\nprint('Need to check actual total_leaves values')\n\"","description":"Analyze tree sizes for backup feasibility"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1400,"cache_read_input_tokens":99572,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1400,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01E1pc1scBgzkR6RxM75HddD","session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"5f0397ed-2406-4ff2-8f55-b0919392f613"}
