8-441\n- The existing small-N fallback path (lines 534-556) already does separate bitrev + tree build with alpha handling\n\nVerify specifically:\n1. Is the alpha handling correct? The fused kernel adds alpha to ALL element q-values. The split approach must do the same.\n2. Does the backup buffer indexing match what `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` would produce after reverting the last tree level?\n3. Is the expected 5-15ms savings realistic given the kernel is 70ms total across 141 instances?\n\nReview this plan following the review guideline. Return your response in the format: Findings, Improvements, Assessment.","agentId":"a38253aae426fb361","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fused bitrev+build kernel takes ~20ms per call for the largest instances. Splitting it into 3 separate passes would add at least 20-30ms overhead (since the fused kernel does all work in one shared-memory pass). This alone would exceed the claimed 5-15ms savings.\n\nNow I have all the evidence needed for the review. Let me write it up.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Memory feasibility blocker: backup buffer requires 16 GB, not 64 MB\n\nThe plan estimates \"N approximately 2M: ~64 MB\" for the backup buffer at APC 300. This is wrong by a factor of 250x. The actual `total_leaves` at APC 300 is approximately 268M per segment (verified from nsight: the two largest `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` instances launch with grid=262144, block=256, which implies `total_leaves / 4 = 67M`, i.e., `total_leaves = 268M`). This matches the metrics: APC 300 has 448M bus interaction messages across 2 segments, giving `total_leaves = 2^28 = 268M` per segment.\n\nA backup of 268M FracExt elements at 64 bytes each = **16.0 GB**. On a 24 GB GPU that already holds the layer buffer (16 GB) plus eq buffers, work buffers, and other allocations, allocating an additional 16 GB backup is not feasible. The `clone_to_new_buffer()` call in Change 1 would OOM.\n\nReference: `fractional.r