{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DzTPP8NccfW6kXyqmgGawY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W8vRfAecFodnNJta8Mzxo4","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-0600-preallocate-whir-buffers-early\n\nPre-allocate WHIR's key device buffers (f_ple_evals, f_coeffs, w_moments) at the start of the STARK proving phase, before the Trace Commit and Constraints phases allocate their temporary buffers. The WHIR phase regressed 28ms (100ms → 128ms at APC 300) after the VPMM page size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB. The regression was attributed to VPMM pool state changes affecting WHIR's bandwidth-bound kernels. By pre-allocating WHIR buffers early (before pool fragmentation from intermediate phases), the buffers may get more favorable physical memory placement, restoring bandwidth characteristics closer to the baseline.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3446,"cache_read_input_tokens":287005,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3446},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"b004f0d7-c493-4a37-96e8-9dc6fa86b288"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W8vRfAecFodnNJta8Mzxo4","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"90049486-2a2d-4c02-963e-64bcdb4afc70","timestamp":"2026-04-16T04:25:05.348Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-0600-preallocate-whir-buffers-early\n\nPre-allocate WHIR's key device buffers (f_ple_evals, f_coeffs, w_moments) at the start of the STARK proving phase, before the Trace Commit and Constraints phases allocate their temporary buffers. The WHIR phase regressed 28ms (100ms → 128ms at APC 300) after the VPMM page size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB. The regression was attributed to VPMM pool state changes affecting WHIR's bandwidth-bound kernels. By pre-allocating WHIR buffers early (before pool fragmentation from intermediate phases), the buffers may get more favorable physical memory placement, restoring bandwidth characteristics closer to the baseline.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-16-0600-eliminate-gkr-revert-field-inversions","+# 2026-04-16-0600-preallocate-whir-buffers-early"," ","-Replace the `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` in GKR fractional sumcheck inner round 0 with a read-only kernel that reads pre-tree-build leaf values from a backup buffer, eliminating the expensive `frac_unadd_inplace` field inversions (one FpExt `inv()` per element). The revert currently performs ~44 Fp multiplications per element (tower-based FpExt reciprocal) to recover the un-combined leaf values from the in-place-built tree. By copying the leaf buffer before the final tree layer is built (~4 MB D2D copy at APC 300), inner round 0 can read the original values directly without field inversions. The `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` accounts for 70ms total across the proof (141 instances) and the revert's field inversions are the dominant per-element cost.","+Pre-allocate WHIR's key device buffers (f_ple_evals, f_coeffs, w_moments) at the start of the STARK proving phase, before the Trace Commit and Constraints phases allocate their temporary buffers. The WHIR phase regressed 28ms (100ms → 128ms at APC 300) after the VPMM page size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB. The regression was attributed to VPMM pool state changes affecting WHIR's bandwidth-bound kernels. By pre-allocating WHIR buffers early (before pool fragmentation from intermediate phases), the buffers may get more favorable physical memory placement, restoring bandwidth characteristics closer to the baseline."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-16-0600-eliminate-gkr-revert-field-inversions\n\nReplace the `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` in GKR fractional sumcheck inner round 0 with a read-only kernel that reads pre-tree-build leaf values from a backup buffer, eliminating the expensive `frac_unadd_inplace` field inversions (one FpExt `inv()` per element). The revert currently performs ~44 Fp multiplications per element (tower-based FpExt reciprocal) to recover the un-combined leaf values from the in-place-built tree. By copying the leaf buffer before the final tree layer is built (~4 MB D2D copy at APC 300), inner round 0 can read the original values directly without field inversions. The `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` accounts for 70ms total across the proof (141 instances) and the revert's field inversions are the dominant per-element cost.\n"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Sf498BQaiFEcAnGqgPY4mP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QK4MzHs1atEwZtREFKvSzy","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0600-preallocate-whir-buffers-early/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Pre-allocate WHIR Buffers Early\n\n## Goal\n\nRecover the 28ms WHIR regression (100ms → 128ms at APC 300) caused by the VPMM page size increase (2 MiB → 16 MiB). The regression is distributed across WHIR's bandwidth-bound kernels (sumcheck, fold, NTT). By pre-allocating WHIR's key buffers at the start of the STARK proving phase — before Trace Commit and Constraints phases fill the cudaMallocAsync pool — the buffers receive early, unfragmented physical memory regions that may have better TLB/cache characteristics.\n\nPrevious attempt: `prealloc-whir-fold-buffers` (2026-04-15-2230) pre-allocated fold scratch buffers INSIDE the WHIR function (after other phases completed). It recovered only 4ms. This plan differs by allocating BEFORE other phases, targeting the pool state hypothesis directly.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### WHIR buffer allocation (`whir.rs:100-195`)\nKey buffers allocated at the start of `prove_whir_opening_gpu`:\n- `f_ple_evals`: `DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(height * D_EF)` — height × 4 F elements\n- `f_coeffs`: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(height)` — height EF elements\n- `w_moments`: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(1 << m)` — same as height EF elements\n- `d_s_evals`: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(2)` — tiny\n- `d_sumcheck_tmp`: grows dynamically per round\n\nAt APC 300, `height = 2^(l_skip + n_stack)` (stacked matrix height). With l_skip=4, the stacked height is determined by the stacking parameters. Each buffer is 2-4 MiB, falling below the 16 MiB VPMM threshold and going through cudaMallocAsync.\n\n### Current allocation timeline\n```\n1. STARK start\n2. Trace Commit: stack_traces (large alloc) → rs_code_matrix (large alloc) → MerkleTree (allocs)\n3. Constraints: per-AIR allocs (Round 0 multi-stream) → GKR buffers → MLE buffers\n4. Openings: Stacked Reduction buffers\n5. WHIR: f_ple_evals, f_coeffs, w_moments allocated HERE (after pool is populated)\n```\n\n### Hypothesis\nThe cudaMallocAsync pool at step 5 has been populated by steps 2-4's alloc/free cycles. The pool's free regions may be physically scattered (different GPU pages, different TLB entries). WHIR's bandwidth-bound kernels (which repeatedly sweep through f_coeffs and w_moments) suffer from TLB thrashing when these buffers span non-contiguous physical pages.\n\nBy allocating at step 1 (before any other allocs), the buffers get contiguous physical regions from the CUDA pool's initial state, restoring the memory layout characteristics from before the VPMM change.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Compute WHIR buffer sizes from proving key\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs`, `prove_openings` or a new helper\n**What**: Before `stark_prove_excluding_trace`, compute the maximum buffer sizes needed for WHIR from the system parameters:\n```rust\nlet whir_height = 1 << (params.l_skip + params.n_stack);\nlet whir_f_ple_size = whir_height * D_EF;  // F elements\nlet whir_ef_size = whir_height;             // EF elements\n```\n\nThese sizes depend only on `SystemParams` (available from the proving key), not on per-segment data.\n\n### Change 2: Pre-allocate WHIR buffers in `Coordinator::prove`\n**File**: `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs`, `prove` method (~line 104)\n**What**: Allocate the 3 key buffers at the start of the function, before `self.device.commit()`:\n\n```rust\nfn prove(&mut self, mpk, unsorted_ctx) -> Result<...> {\n    // Pre-allocate WHIR buffers before any other device allocations\n    let whir_buffers = self.device.preallocate_whir_buffers(mpk)?;\n    \n    // ... existing code: commit, transcript, prove_constraints, prove_openings\n    // Pass whir_buffers to prove_openings\n}\n```\n\n### Change 3: Add `WhirPreallocBuffers` struct\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` (new struct)\n**What**:\n```rust\npub struct WhirPreallocBuffers {\n    pub f_ple_evals: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n    pub f_coeffs: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    pub w_moments: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n}\n```\n\n### Change 4: Add `preallocate_whir_buffers` to `GpuDevice`\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs`\n**What**: Compute sizes from `SystemParams` and allocate:\n```rust\nfn preallocate_whir_buffers(&self, mpk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<...>) -> Result<WhirPreallocBuffers, ProverError> {\n    let height = 1 << (self.config.l_skip + self.config.n_stack);\n    Ok(WhirPreallocBuffers {\n        f_ple_evals: DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(height * D_EF),\n        f_coeffs: DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(height),\n        w_moments: DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(height),\n    })\n}\n```\n\n### Change 5: Thread pre-allocated buffers to WHIR\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs`, `prove_openings` method\n**What**: Pass `whir_buffers` through `prove_openings` → `prove_whir_opening_gpu`.\n\nThis requires adding `whir_buffers: WhirPreallocBuffers` parameter to:\n- `OpeningProver::prove_openings` (trait method in `hal.rs`)\n- `prove_whir_opening_gpu` (in `whir.rs`)\n\n### Change 6: Use pre-allocated buffers in WHIR\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`, `prove_whir_opening_gpu`\n**What**: Replace the 3 allocations at lines 100, 148, 175 with the pre-allocated buffers:\n\n```rust\n// Before:\nlet mut f_ple_evals = DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(height * D_EF);\n// After:\nlet mut f_ple_evals = whir_buffers.f_ple_evals;\nassert!(f_ple_evals.len() >= height * D_EF);\n\n// Similar for f_coeffs and w_moments\n```\n\n### Change 7: Handle per-segment reuse\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`\n**What**: For multi-segment proofs, the pre-allocated buffers must be reused across segments. After each segment's WHIR phase completes, the buffers are returned to the caller for the next segment. The `WhirPreallocBuffers` should be passed by `&mut` or returned:\n\n```rust\nfn prove_whir_opening_gpu(..., whir_buffers: &mut WhirPreallocBuffers) -> ...\n```\n\nThe function takes ownership of the buffer data at the start and writes it back at the end, or uses the buffers in-place via pointer/len access.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Buffer sizes**: The pre-allocated buffers must be at least as large as what WHIR would allocate. Since `height` is determined by `l_skip + n_stack` (system parameters), and these don't change between segments, the size is correct for all segments.\n2. **Buffer contents**: The pre-allocated buffers are written to by WHIR (f_ple_evals is filled by algebraic batching, etc.). The pre-allocation only controls WHERE in physical memory the buffers land, not their contents. Correctness is unchanged.\n3. **Memory lifetime**: The buffers are allocated before Trace Commit and held until WHIR completes. This increases peak GPU memory by ~12 MiB (3 buffers × 4 MiB). Within the 24 GB budget.\n4. **Multi-segment**: Buffers are reused across segments (same size, overwritten each segment).\n5. **Trait changes**: The `OpeningProver` trait needs a mechanism to pass pre-allocated buffers. This could be via a generic associated type or by making the buffers part of `GpuDevice` state.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC 0, 100, 300\n2. Compare `WHIR` and `STARK excl trace` metrics\n3. Expected: WHIR at APC 300 recovers 10-28ms (from 128ms toward 100ms baseline)\n4. Expected: No regression at APC 0 (WHIR is ~44ms/segment, unchanged)\n5. If WHIR doesn't improve: the hypothesis (pool state / physical memory layout) is wrong\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Revert if WHIR at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n- Revert if any APC configuration shows regression > 20ms\n- Revert if any correctness failure\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8634,"cache_read_input_tokens":290451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8634},"output_tokens":69,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"30b0d060-34e7-4215-b7c1-df83442fc1c3"}
