{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017LhMwbwQXt3esWDPSiy5QK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: VPMM Bulk Page Creation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate the ~58ms cold-start overhead in the first segment's `rs_code_matrix` allocation at APC 300 by batching the VPMM's physical page creation. Currently, `defragment_or_create_new_pages` creates ~128 pages one at a time (each a separate `cuMemCreate` + `cuMemMap` driver call). This plan replaces the per-page `cuMemCreate` loop with a single bulk `cuMemCreate` call for the total needed size, then maps individual page-sized chunks from the bulk allocation using offset-based `cuMemMap`.\n6\t\n7\tThis is fundamentally different from the failed pool-warming approach (`cache-codeword-buffer-across-segments`), which allocated and freed a buffer before proving, creating a free region that disrupted VPMM pool state and caused a 177ms GKR regression at APC 0. The bulk page creation approach does not create or free any buffer — it changes only the internal mechanics of how physical pages are created when the pool genuinely needs to grow. The resulting pool state (VA addresses, page locations, free regions) is identical to today.\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t### Allocation trigger\n12\t\n13\t1. `stacked_commit()` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:48` calls `rs_code_matrix()` at line 70.\n14\t2. `rs_code_matrix()` at line 182 allocates `DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(codeword_height * width)` at line 194. At APC 300 seg 0, this is ~192MB.\n15\t3. `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity` calls into the VPMM via `MEMORY_MANAGER.lock().alloc()`.\n16\t\n17\t### VPMM allocation flow\n18\t\n19\t4. `VirtualMemoryPool::alloc()` (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs:200-270`) looks for a free region. On cold start, none exist.\n20\t5. Calls `defragment_or_create_new_pages(alloc_size, stream_id)` at line 217.\n21\t6. `defragment_or_create_new_pages()` (line 510) computes `allocate_size` = bytes needing new pages.\n22\t7. **The hot loop** (lines 538-576): creates pages one at a time:\n23\t   ```\n24\t   while allocated_dst < dst + allocate_size {\n25\t       handle = vpmm_create_physical(device_id, page_size)  // cuMemCreate — ~0.4ms each\n26\t       vpmm_map(allocated_dst, page_size, handle)            // cuMemMap   — ~0.05ms each\n27\t       active_pages.insert(allocated_dst, handle)\n28\t       allocated_dst += page_size\n29\t   }\n30\t   ```\n31\t   With page_size=2MB and allocate_size=256MB: 128 iterations × ~0.45ms = ~58ms.\n32\t8. After the loop, a single `vpmm_set_access()` call sets read-write permissions on the entire range.\n33\t\n34\t### Page tracking and lifecycle\n35\t\n36\t- `active_pages: HashMap<CUdeviceptr, CUmemGenericAllocationHandle>` maps each page's VA to its physical allocation handle. Currently, each page has a unique handle.\n37\t- Pages are never released during normal operation — only on pool `Drop` or allocation rollback.\n38\t- `remap_regions()` (line 687) double-maps pages during defragmentation, looking up each page's handle via `active_pages[va]` and calling `vpmm_map(new_va, page_size, handle)` with implicit offset=0.\n39\t- `Drop` (line 757) iterates `active_pages` and calls `vpmm_unmap(va, page_size)` + `vpmm_release(handle)` per page.\n40\t- `rollback_new_pages()` (line 481) does the same for partially-created allocations on failure.\n41\t\n42\t### Why the current code is slow\n43\t\n44\t`cuMemCreate` (called via `vpmm_create_physical`) is the dominant cost at ~0.4ms per call because it interacts with the GPU physical memory allocator. With 128 calls, total `cuMemCreate` time is ~51ms. `cuMemMap` is cheap (~0.05ms each) because it only updates the GPU page table.\n45\t\n46\t### CUDA API detail\n47\t\n48\tThe `cuMemMap(ptr, size, offset, handle, flags)` API supports an `offset` parameter that maps a sub-range of a physical allocation. The current C shim (`crates/cuda-common/cuda/src/vpmm_shim.cu:53`) hardcodes offset=0:\n49\t```c\n50\tint _vpmm_map(CUdeviceptr va, size_t bytes, CUmemGenericAllocationHandle h) {"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J17JjUQ3eVEaLrWxZRQ1KK","session_id":"80cd739d-0408-463a-95a7-972cb66aaf37","uuid":"460645f2-e10a-4d6d-bd21-d4cb1350fc54","timestamp":"2026-04-16T04:35:44.930Z"}
